 Here’s how pi works and how you can turn it into a web-backed coding agent.

## What pi is

pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`) is a **minimal, extensible coding agent**. Out of the box it gives an LLM tools like `read`, `write`, `edit`, `bash`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`. The core is small; most behavior is added through **extensions**, **skills**, **prompt templates**, **themes**, and **pi packages**.

It can run in four modes:

| Mode | Best for |
|------|----------|
| **Interactive (TUI)** | Terminal chat |
| **Print (`-p`)** | One-shot CLI output |
| **JSON (`--mode json`)** | Structured event output |
| **RPC (`--mode rpc`)** | Subprocess integration |
| **SDK** | Embedding in your own Node.js app |

For a web server, you want either the **SDK** (same Node.js process) or **RPC** (spawn `pi` as a child process). SDK is the better fit for what you described.

---

## Extension / plugin mechanism

Extensions are TypeScript modules that export a default factory:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  // Lifecycle + agent events
  pi.on("tool_call", async (event, ctx) => { ... });
  pi.on("agent_start", async () => { ... });

  // Register LLM-callable tools
  pi.registerTool({ name: "deploy", ... });

  // Register slash commands
  pi.registerCommand("status", { ... });

  // Register custom model providers
  pi.registerProvider("my-proxy", { ... });
}
```

They are loaded via [jiti](https://github.com/unjs/jiti), so no build step is required. Auto-discovery paths:

- Global: `~/.pi/agent/extensions/*.ts`
- Project-local: `.pi/extensions/*.ts`
- Settings: `settings.json` → `"extensions": [...]`
- Packages: `pi install npm:...` / `pi install git:...`

What extensions can do:

- **Custom tools** (or replace built-ins)
- **Event interception** (`tool_call`, `tool_result`, `context`, `before_provider_request`, etc.)
- **Custom UI** (`ctx.ui.select/confirm/input`, widgets, status)
- **Custom providers** (proxy, OAuth, private endpoints)
- **Session control** (fork, compact, new session)
- **Permission gates**, git checkpointing, MCP integration, etc.

---

## Using pi as a web-server harness

The cleanest architecture is a **Node.js web server that uses the pi SDK directly**. This keeps the agent runtime, tools, event streaming, and session state in-process, which is much simpler than managing a fleet of `pi --mode rpc` child processes.

### High-level design

```
Browser (React/Vue/Svelte)
  ↕ WebSocket or SSE
Node.js server (Hono / Express / Fastify)
  ↕ pi SDK
    createAgentSession({ cwd, modelRuntime, sessionManager, resourceLoader })
    session.subscribe(event => forward to client)
    session.prompt(message)
```

Each chat session maps to one `AgentSession`. You can persist sessions to disk with `SessionManager.create(cwd)` or keep them in-memory with `SessionManager.inMemory()`.

### Minimal server sketch

```ts
import { createAgentSession, ModelRuntime, SessionManager } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { Hono } from "hono";
import { createMiddleware } from "hono/factory";

const app = new Hono();
const modelRuntime = await ModelRuntime.create();

// Auth middleware (replace with your own)
const auth = createMiddleware(async (c, next) => {
  const token = c.req.header("authorization");
  if (!isValid(token)) return c.json({ error: "unauthorized" }, 401);
  await next();
});

// Map sessionId -> AgentSession
const sessions = new Map<string, AgentSession>();

app.post("/sessions/:id/prompt", auth, async (c) => {
  const sessionId = c.req.param("id");
  const { message } = await c.req.json();

  let session = sessions.get(sessionId);
  if (!session) {
    const created = await createAgentSession({
      modelRuntime,
      sessionManager: SessionManager.inMemory(),
      cwd: "/path/to/project",
    });
    session = created.session;
    sessions.set(sessionId, session);

    // Forward events to your pub/sub or WebSocket room
    session.subscribe((event) => {
      broadcast(sessionId, event);
    });
  }

  await session.prompt(message);
  return c.json({ ok: true });
});

app.post("/sessions/:id/abort", auth, async (c) => {
  sessions.get(c.req.param("id"))?.abort();
  return c.json({ ok: true });
});
```

### Streaming events to the browser

`session.subscribe()` gives you every event: `message_update` (text/token deltas), `tool_execution_start/end`, `agent_start/end`, `turn_start/end`, `compaction_start/end`, etc. Forward these over **WebSocket** or **SSE** and render them in your UI.

Example event handling:

```ts
session.subscribe((event) => {
  if (event.type === "message_update" && event.assistantMessageEvent.type === "text_delta") {
    // stream text chunk to browser
    send({ type: "text", delta: event.assistantMessageEvent.delta });
  }
  if (event.type === "tool_execution_start") {
    send({ type: "tool_start", tool: event.toolName, args: event.args });
  }
  if (event.type === "tool_execution_end") {
    send({ type: "tool_end", result: event.result, isError: event.isError });
  }
});
```

### Tools “just work”

When you use `createAgentSession`, the LLM gets the built-in tools automatically. If you want extra tools, you can:

1. **Inline custom tools** via `customTools`:
   ```ts
   const myTool = defineTool({ name: "my_tool", ... });
   await createAgentSession({ customTools: [myTool] });
   ```

2. **Load extensions** via `DefaultResourceLoader`:
   ```ts
   const loader = new DefaultResourceLoader({
     cwd: projectPath,
     additionalExtensionPaths: ["./server-extensions/"],
     extensionFactories: [{ name: "web-ui-bridge", factory: (pi) => { ... } }],
   });
   await loader.reload();
   await createAgentSession({ resourceLoader: loader });
   ```

3. **Register a provider proxy** in an extension if you need to route LLM calls through your own backend.

---

## Important security considerations

Since you’re exposing this behind a web page:

1. **pi tools have full filesystem/shell access.** `bash`, `write`, `edit`, etc. can execute arbitrary commands. Run the server in a sandbox (container, VM, restricted user, readonly mounts) unless you fully trust every user.
2. **Extensions run as the server process.** Any loaded extension can execute arbitrary code. Only load extensions you trust.
3. **Session isolation.** Make sure each web user/session gets its own `AgentSession` and `cwd`, or users can see/overwrite each other’s work.
4. **Authentication.** pi itself has no auth; that’s your web server’s job. Gate `/prompt`, `/sessions`, etc. with your existing auth.
5. **Resource limits.** Set command timeouts, output truncation, and consider disabling `bash` or replacing it with a restricted tool for untrusted users.

---

## Alternative: RPC mode

If you don’t want the SDK in-process, you can spawn `pi --mode rpc` per session and speak JSONL over stdin/stdout:

```ts
const proc = spawn("pi", ["--mode", "rpc", "--no-session"], { cwd: projectPath });
proc.stdin.write(JSON.stringify({ type: "prompt", message: "Hello" }) + "\n");
```

This is language-agnostic but harder to scale (one OS process per session, stdout/stderr management, process lifecycle). SDK is generally preferred for a web backend.

