  Yes — this is exactly what pi extensions are designed for. You can build a **permission-gate extension** that intercepts tool calls and either blocks them or routes them through an approval UI. You can also do it at multiple layers for defense in depth.

## Where pi lets you gate things

### 1. `tool_call` event — block/allow individual tool invocations

The most direct hook. In an extension:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI, ExtensionContext } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { isToolCallEventType } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  pi.on("tool_call", async (event, ctx) => {
    // Always allow read-only tools
    if (["read", "grep", "find", "ls"].includes(event.toolName)) {
      return;
    }

    // Block dangerous patterns even before prompting
    if (event.toolName === "bash") {
      const cmd = (event.input as { command?: string }).command ?? "";
      if (/\brm\s+-rf\b/.test(cmd)) {
        return { block: true, reason: "rm -rf is not allowed", terminate: true };
      }
    }

    // For everything else, ask for approval
    const approved = await ctx.ui.confirm(
      `Allow ${event.toolName}?`,
      JSON.stringify(event.input, null, 2)
    );

    if (!approved) {
      return { block: true, reason: "User denied tool call" };
    }
  });
}
```

`{ block: true }` prevents execution. `terminate: true` stops the agent turn if this was a terminating call.

### 2. `tool_result` event — inspect/modify results

If you want to redact secrets or add warnings after execution:

```ts
pi.on("tool_result", async (event, ctx) => {
  if (event.toolName === "read" && event.input.path.includes(".env")) {
    return {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: "[redacted: .env contents hidden by policy]" }],
      isError: false,
    };
  }
});
```

### 3. Tool allowlists at session creation

The SDK lets you restrict which tools are even exposed to the model:

```ts
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  tools: ["read", "grep", "find", "ls"], // only these built-ins
  excludeTools: ["bash", "write", "edit"],
});
```

This is a hard allowlist — the model literally cannot call anything else. Combine this with extension-level gates for the tools you do expose.

### 4. Custom replacement tools

If you don’t trust the built-in `bash`, you can **replace it** with a restricted version:

```ts
pi.on("tool_call", async (event, ctx) => {
  if (event.toolName === "bash") {
    // Block the real bash entirely
    return { block: true, reason: "bash is disabled; use sandbox_bash instead" };
  }
});

pi.registerTool({
  name: "sandbox_bash",
  label: "Sandbox Bash",
  description: "Run commands in a restricted sandbox",
  parameters: Type.Object({
    command: Type.String(),
  }),
  execute: async (_id, params, signal, onUpdate, ctx) => {
    // Run only through an allowlisted runner, e.g. `bwrap`, `firejail`, Docker
    const result = await runInSandbox(params.command, ctx.cwd);
    return { content: [{ type: "text", text: result.stdout }], details: {} };
  },
});
```

