## How the model learns about tools

When you register a tool, pi includes it in the system prompt with:

- **Name** — what the model calls, e.g. `presentation_update`
- **Description** — when to use it and what it does
- **Parameters schema** — JSON Schema (via `typebox`) describing inputs

The model sees something like:

```json
{
  "name": "presentation_update",
  "description": "Modify the live presentation. Use this when the user asks to change layout, styling, or content of slides. The presentation editor and pi share the same state.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "action": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["setPosition", "setSize", "setText", "setFillColor", "setFontSize", "applyGridLayout"],
        "description": "The operation to perform"
      },
      "targetIds": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": { "type": "string" },
        "description": "IDs of the selected objects to modify"
      },
      "args": {
        "type": "object",
        "description": "Action-specific parameters"
      }
    },
    "required": ["action", "targetIds"]
  }
}
```

The model then decides to call `presentation_update` based on the user prompt and the tool description.

